        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Interface Pollution"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Mirroring every struct with a same-shaped interface hurts",
            "Navigation and refactoring both get worse",
            "Premature interfaces add indirection with no payoff"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Payoff Patterns"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "go",
          "lines": [
            "io.Copy(dst Writer, src Reader) // any pair",
            "func Logging(n http.Handler) http.Handler",
            "// one method = composable middleware"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Capability upgrade: assert for `io.ReaderFrom` or `http.Flusher`",
            "A one-method seam mocks with a func-typed adapter",
            "A hand-written fake beats a generated mock for readability"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Lang",
            "Satisfy",
            "Shape"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Go",
              "implicit",
              "consumer, small"
            ],
            [
              "Java/C#",
              "`implements`",
              "producer, wide"
            ],
            [
              "Rust",
              "`impl` trait",
              "foreign OK, static"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Interface or type parameter?",
          "text": "Interface when behaviour varies at run time. Type parameter when the type is known at compile time and you want no boxing."
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Document The Contract",
          "items": [
            "Blocking or not? Reentrant?",
            "Who closes, who owns the buffer?",
            "The compiler checks shape only, never semantics"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Costs and Trade-offs",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Where The Cost Really Is",
          "ink": "accent"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Indirect call through the itab: typically a few nanoseconds",
            "The bigger loss is that the callee can no longer inline",
            "Lost inlining is what pushes values to the heap"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Boxing"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Assigning a non-pointer value to an interface may allocate",
            "Check with `-benchmem` and `-gcflags=-m`",
            "Free cases: pointers, cached small ints, devirtualized calls"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "small value",
              "boxed in iface",
              "heap object",
              "GC scan work"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Call kind",
            "Inlining",
            "Alloc"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "direct",
              "yes",
              "none"
            ],
            [
              "interface",
              "usually no",
              "possible box"
            ],
            [
              "generic",
              "often yes",
              "per GC shape"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Containers And GC"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "An `interface{}` container costs one allocation per element",
            "Plus one assertion per read — generics avoid both",
            "Many boxed values means many tiny objects to scan"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Assertion And Switch Cost"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Concrete assertion: a single pointer comparison",
            "Interface to interface goes through `getitab`, cached",
            "A type switch compiles to a hash jump, not a linear scan"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Optimising Honestly"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sh",
          "lines": [
            "go test -bench=Iface -benchmem",
            "go build -pgo=default.pgo ./...",
            "# PGO 1.21+ devirtualizes hot calls"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Hoist the assertion out of the innermost loop",
            "Avoid interfaces inside a hot path's inner loop",
            "Do not de-interface preemptively: readability usually wins"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "`i1 == i2` can panic",
          "text": "Comparison checks type and value. If the held type is a slice, map or func it panics at run time."
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Map Key Hazard",
          "items": [
            "Interfaces as map keys inherit that hazard",
            "`panic: runtime error: hash of unhashable type []int`",
            "Use a comparable concrete key type instead"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "The Typed-Nil Trap and Gotchas 2-5",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Typed-Nil Trap",
          "ink": "accent"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "go",
          "lines": [
            "var p *MyErr           // nil",
            "var e error = p",
            "if e != nil { /* TRUE. always. */ }"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Never return a nil concrete pointer as an `error`",
            "Return the literal `nil` instead",
            "Or: `if p != nil { return p }; return nil`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Why It Happens"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "An interface value is two words: ==type== plus ==data==",
            "`e = p` writes `*MyErr` into the type word",
            "`e == nil` holds only when **both** words are unset"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "go",
          "lines": [
            "func f() error {",
            "\tp := find()   // *MyErr or nil",
            "\tif p != nil { return p }",
            "\treturn nil    // both words nil",
            "}"
          ],
          "note": "never return the typed pointer"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "The one-line rule",
          "text": "A concrete pointer type as a return type leaks typed nils. Declare the return as `error` and return literal `nil`."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Four More From The List"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
